00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:02.167
Or as Heather Anne Campbell tweeted,

00:01:02.167 --> 00:01:03.737
"Can't believe the end of 'Joker'

00:01:03.737 --> 00:01:04.767
"is Joaquin Phoenix

00:01:04.767 --> 00:01:06.369
"turning to camera and saying,

00:01:06.369 --> 00:01:08.230
"'Now you do Joker stuff.'"

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:10.730
In contrast, Adam McKay's "Don't Look Up"

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:11.951
was widely applauded,

00:01:11.951 --> 00:01:13.630
but generally more so

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:15.830
for its moral appeal that we should,

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:18.730
you know, save the dying rock we live on,

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:21.350
rather than for its actual artistic merit.

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:22.570
This led many bristle

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:23.660
at the implication that

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:24.900
if you don't like the movie,

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:27.350
you don't care about climate change.

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:29.070
All of this has us wondering,

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:30.980
does good art need to be moral?

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:32.310
And when our culture debates

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:33.431
the morality of art,

00:01:33.431 --> 00:01:35.720
are we asking the right questions?

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:36.966
Let's dive into this totally

00:01:36.966 --> 00:01:38.900
not contentious issue

