And now, back to the show.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:11.500
Now philosophers, critics and artists

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:12.820
have been debating the tension

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:13.999
between art and morality

00:03:13.999 --> 00:03:16.000
since Ancient Greek times.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:18.130
What's more, cultural hand wringing

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:19.390
about immoral art

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:21.780
turning us all into unprincipled hooligans

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:22.730
is nothing new.

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:24.010
For much of history,

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:26.240
art has actually been heavily controlled

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:28.220
by each era's moral authorities.

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:29.638
During the Middle Ages in Europe,

00:03:29.638 --> 00:03:32.020
painters saw aesthetics crushed

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:34.250
by what scholar FB Chambers calls

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:36.330
the Christian Moral Resistance.

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:40.180
Like the infamous bonfire
of the vanities in 1497,

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:42.637
where an incensed, fanatical Italian monk

00:03:42.637 --> 00:03:45.410
convinced mobs to burn everything

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:47.410
he found "heretical."

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:49.250
From books to artwork,

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:51.130
to musical instruments, even makeup.

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:53.457
It's theorized that
famous painter Botticelli

00:03:53.457 --> 00:03:54.936
was even persuaded to burn

00:03:54.936 --> 00:03:57.398
some of his own paintings

00:03:57.398 --> 00:03:59.380
that were considered heretical.

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:00.300
In the Renaissance,

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:01.480
things changed a bit.

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:03.410
Painters like Leonardo da Vinci

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:05.057
defined their artistic duty

00:04:05.057 --> 00:04:07.560
as uniting aesthetics and morality

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:09.690
into beautiful moral art.

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:12.130
But The Enlightenment changed everything.

00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:14.100
The free thinking, more secular era

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:15.470
saw European artists

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:18.250
largely emancipated from moral policing.

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:20.790
What followed, as scholar
Arnold Berlant writes,

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:23.060
was "an aberration in arts history"

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:26.960
as artists finally became
basically autonomous.

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:29.360
Of course, this freedom
left some disgruntled.

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:30.216
And over the centuries,

00:04:30.216 --> 00:04:32.440
has led to artists like DH Lawrence

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:33.620
being taken to court

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:36.030
over their art's supposed immorality.

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:37.769
Though his publisher, Penguin Books,

00:04:37.769 --> 00:04:39.107
was the one on trial

00:04:39.107 --> 00:04:41.100
and we can only hope Mr. Penguin

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:42.810
was forced to take the stand.

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:43.930
Critiques of immoral art

00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:45.838
have continued into the modern day.

00:04:45.838 --> 00:04:48.270
Typically surfacing as moral panics

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:50.090
over the art children are consuming.

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:51.560
Rock n' roll was going to turn

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:54.030
the teens into sex monsters in the 50s.

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:55.040
and the 80s and 90s

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:57.210
saw major hysteria about violence

00:04:57.210 --> 00:05:00.150
in rap, video games,
action movies, and so on.

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:02.380
Surely, kids were being prepped

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:03.700
for lives of crime.

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:06.010
Of course, crime rates plummeted.

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:08.510
Straight up plummeted in the 90s.

00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:10.520
But why let facts get in the way

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:12.620
of a good old fashioned moral panic.

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:13.900
At the same time,

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:15.200
there's been a recent reckoning

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:16.040
with some of history's

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:18.720
more questionable works of art.

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:20.200
Take "Birth of a Nation,"

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:21.420
which was once hailed

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:22.890
as a an astonishing feat

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:24.760
of early American filmmaking.

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:26.300
Today, it is better known

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:27.790
for almost single handedly

00:05:27.790 --> 00:05:29.630
reviving the KKK.

00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:31.000
And today, the tension

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:32.946
between art and morality lingers.

00:05:32.946 --> 00:05:35.240
Artists aren't being explicitly policed

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:36.410
by, say, the church.

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:37.243
At the same time,

00:05:37.243 --> 00:05:38.410
they are subject

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:39.810
to an increasingly loud

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:41.500
court of public opinion that,

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:43.005
especially on social media,

00:05:43.005 --> 00:05:44.890
is often quite critical

00:05:44.890 --> 00:05:47.160
of art's perceived moral shortcomings.

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:48.630
Take the low key hysterics

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:50.350
surrounding the release of "The Hunt,"

00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:52.650
which re-imagined
America's political divide

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:53.900
as hide and seek,

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:55.460
but add shotguns.

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:56.293
With all this,

00:05:56.293 --> 00:05:58.240
we think the real overarching question

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:01.067
when it comes to ethical
criticism of art is,

00:06:01.067 --> 00:06:03.587
"Does the implied ethics of a work of art

00:06:03.587 --> 00:06:05.470
"affect its aesthetic value?"

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:07.660
Scholar ‪Noel Carroll‬ characterized

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:09.410
the two main opposing sides

00:06:09.410 --> 00:06:10.980
in the debate as such.

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:13.268
On one side there's autonomism.

00:06:13.268 --> 00:06:14.101
This is the view that,

00:06:14.101 --> 00:06:15.207
as Carroll writes,

00:06:15.207 --> 00:06:18.097
"Art and ethics are
autonomous realms of value

00:06:18.097 --> 00:06:20.417
"and, thus, criteria
from the ethical realm

00:06:20.417 --> 00:06:21.607
"should not be imported

00:06:21.607 --> 00:06:23.870
"to evaluate the aesthetic realm."

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:25.710
This is also called aestheticism

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:27.140
and its adherents argue

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:28.990
that art should be considered valuable

00:06:28.990 --> 00:06:30.850
for its aesthetic qualities,

00:06:30.850 --> 00:06:31.940
not for its ability

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:34.650
to morally enlighten or improve us.

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:36.327
As proponent Oscar Wilde put it,

00:06:36.327 --> 00:06:39.517
"There is no such thing as
a moral or immoral book.

00:06:39.517 --> 00:06:41.687
"Books are well written or badly written.

00:06:41.687 --> 00:06:42.600
"That is all."

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:44.930
More commonly, moderate autonomism

00:06:44.930 --> 00:06:46.250
holds that it's possible

00:06:46.250 --> 00:06:47.895
to morally evaluate art,

00:06:47.895 --> 00:06:49.690
but it's just not relevant

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:51.085
to whether that art is actually

00:06:51.085 --> 00:06:53.340
good or bad aesthetically speaking.

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:55.350
On the other side, there's moralism.

00:06:55.350 --> 00:06:56.230
This is the view that

00:06:56.230 --> 00:06:58.650
if a work of art has immoral implications,

00:06:58.650 --> 00:07:00.247
those implications contribute

00:07:00.247 --> 00:07:02.070
to its aesthetic defects.

00:07:02.070 --> 00:07:04.650
Therefore, the aesthetic value of art

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:07.210
can be compromised by its immorality.

00:07:07.210 --> 00:07:09.840
Now, let's take a closer
look at each side.

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:12.800
Autonomism has quite a
philosophical history.

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:14.400
The likes of Hume and Kant

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:15.613
believed that we have to separate

00:07:15.613 --> 00:07:18.360
aesthetic and moral faculties.

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:19.880
Philosopher Francis Hutcheson

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:21.260
considered those faculties

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:23.180
distinctive enough to be considered

00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:24.860
like different senses.

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:27.210
As in, hearing versus seeing.

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:28.043
Just like you can't

00:07:28.043 --> 00:07:30.050
use your ears to evaluate the crumb

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:31.150
on this week's Bake Off,

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:32.740
you can't use your moral compass

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:33.810
to evaluate art.

00:07:33.810 --> 00:07:35.710
Kant believed that what we consider

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:38.700
pleasurable aesthetically is our taste.

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:40.765
And what we consider pleasurable morally

00:07:40.765 --> 00:07:43.490
is simply our moral feeling.

00:07:43.490 --> 00:07:44.690
In more recent years,

00:07:44.690 --> 00:07:47.100
scholar Clive Bell has argued that art,

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:48.470
or at least visual art,

00:07:48.470 --> 00:07:50.980
needs to satisfy two essential features.

00:07:50.980 --> 00:07:52.060
It provokes a particular

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:53.500
sort of emotional response

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:55.110
and that emotional response

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:56.326
is the proper basis

00:07:56.326 --> 00:07:58.340
of its aesthetic evaluation.

00:07:58.340 --> 00:07:59.930
Once we've judge something is art,

00:07:59.930 --> 00:08:02.360
he argued, morality becomes about

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:05.050
as relevant as a piano is to a pig.

00:08:05.050 --> 00:08:07.327
He notes that as, "Art is above morals,

00:08:07.327 --> 00:08:09.157
"or, rather, all art is moral

00:08:09.157 --> 00:08:10.357
"because works of art

00:08:10.357 --> 00:08:12.047
"are immediate means to good.

00:08:12.047 --> 00:08:14.237
"Once we have judged a
thing of work of art,

00:08:14.237 --> 00:08:15.637
"we have judged it ethically

00:08:15.637 --> 00:08:17.327
"of the first importance

00:08:17.327 --> 00:08:19.627
"and put it beyond the
reach of the moralist."

00:08:19.627 --> 00:08:20.625
Under this view,

00:08:20.625 --> 00:08:22.020
you could judge "Joker"

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:23.620
as a work of art for engendering

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:24.647
the emotions of, say,

00:08:24.647 --> 00:08:28.300
fear, anxiety, or coulrophobia,

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:29.850
i.e. fear of clowns

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:31.000
that go bump in the night.

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:32.210
This seems fair.

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:33.537
Thus Joker's moral message

00:08:33.537 --> 00:08:35.430
is rendered irrelevant

00:08:35.430 --> 00:08:37.790
and the work is beyond ethical reproach.

00:08:37.790 --> 00:08:38.790
As Bell puts it,

00:08:38.790 --> 00:08:40.580
we should "liberate the artist"

00:08:40.580 --> 00:08:41.930
from moral judgment.

00:08:41.930 --> 00:08:44.170
Then there's moralism,

00:08:44.170 --> 00:08:45.060
which has dominated

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:46.690
for most of human history.

00:08:46.690 --> 00:08:48.770
Carroll notes that before
the wedge was driven

00:08:48.770 --> 00:08:50.410
between ethics and aesthetics

00:08:50.410 --> 00:08:51.780
by Hutcheson and Kant,

00:08:51.780 --> 00:08:53.825
ethical criticism of art was common

00:08:53.825 --> 00:08:56.500
and thought of as totally appropriate.

00:08:56.500 --> 00:08:58.630
Prominently, there was Plato's criticism

00:08:58.630 --> 00:09:00.890
of music and poetry in Republic,

00:09:00.890 --> 00:09:03.650
which he believed was making kids naughty.

00:09:03.650 --> 00:09:04.880
His argument culminates

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:06.515
with Socrates saying that poets,

00:09:06.515 --> 00:09:09.216
including mother effin' Homer,

00:09:09.216 --> 00:09:11.720
would be banned from the ideal city.

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:13.010
In the late 19th century,

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:15.270
Leo Tolstoy leaned into moralism,

00:09:15.270 --> 00:09:16.850
rejecting aesthetic theories

00:09:16.850 --> 00:09:18.040
that focused on beauty.

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:20.960
He complained that
upper class European art

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:24.090
got bad when it stopped being religious.

00:09:24.090 --> 00:09:25.745
And fretted that the resulting art

00:09:25.745 --> 00:09:27.631
directly corrupts people

00:09:27.631 --> 00:09:29.647
"by infecting them with the worst

00:09:29.647 --> 00:09:31.675
"and humanly most harmful feelings.

00:09:31.675 --> 00:09:34.230
"Chiefly, of sensuality."

00:09:34.230 --> 00:09:36.437
And he never even lived to read

00:09:36.437 --> 00:09:37.620
"50 Shades of Gray."

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:39.190
He draws an analogy with food.

00:09:39.190 --> 00:09:40.952
People may eat food for pleasure,

00:09:40.952 --> 00:09:43.850
but its real function is nourishment.

00:09:43.850 --> 00:09:46.610
Likewise, people may
consume art for pleasure,

00:09:46.610 --> 00:09:47.980
but its real function

00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:49.290
is to communicate.

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:52.020
Specifically, to communicate emotions.

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:53.490
The more the art infects you

00:09:53.490 --> 00:09:54.404
with those emotions,

00:09:54.404 --> 00:09:56.470
the better the art is,

00:09:56.470 --> 00:09:58.161
so long as those emotions are

00:09:58.161 --> 00:10:00.360
the ones valued by Tolstoy's

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:02.010
humanist brand of Christianity.

00:10:02.010 --> 00:10:03.241
Deep religious feeling,

00:10:03.241 --> 00:10:05.720
horror and injustice, and so on.

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:07.700
Moral virtue and artistic virtue

00:10:07.700 --> 00:10:09.590
become one and the same.

00:10:09.590 --> 00:10:11.220
Some moralists point to art

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:13.420
that is pretty objectively

00:10:13.420 --> 00:10:14.500
morally troubling

00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:15.837
to support their argument.

00:10:15.837 --> 00:10:16.900
"Triumph of the Will"

00:10:16.900 --> 00:10:18.080
is a common example.

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:19.180
Made by the Nazi state's

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:21.800
favorite director, Lenny Riefenstahl,

00:10:21.800 --> 00:10:23.270
the movie unabashedly

00:10:23.270 --> 00:10:25.380
worships Hitler and his ideology.

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:27.060
It is at the same time

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:28.440
an aesthetically creative

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:30.360
and groundbreaking work of cinema.

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:31.370
But moralists argue

00:10:31.370 --> 00:10:32.780
that any aesthetic achievements

00:10:32.780 --> 00:10:33.916
are sullied by the film's

00:10:33.916 --> 00:10:36.360
objectively (beep) worldview.

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:38.185
Now, nobody wants to be the guy

00:10:38.185 --> 00:10:41.410
who argues in favor of
"Triumph of the Will."

00:10:41.410 --> 00:10:42.865
But at first blush,

00:10:42.865 --> 00:10:45.450
we do feel like the autonomists

00:10:45.450 --> 00:10:46.440
make a good point.

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:49.673
After all, didactic art
with a moral worldview

00:10:49.673 --> 00:10:52.020
can have little aesthetic value.

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:53.540
Takes something like "The Blindside,"

00:10:53.540 --> 00:10:55.540
which takes the morally correct stance

00:10:55.540 --> 00:10:56.373
on the question of,

00:10:56.373 --> 00:10:57.570
"Should we be racist?"

00:10:57.570 --> 00:10:58.673
While at the same time,

00:10:58.673 --> 00:11:02.100
offering very little in
the realm of aesthetics.

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:04.560
What's more, lots of good abstract art

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:06.302
has plenty of aesthetic value,

00:11:06.302 --> 00:11:08.570
but has no objective moral value.

00:11:08.570 --> 00:11:09.810
Carroll calls the argument

00:11:09.810 --> 00:11:11.190
regarding abstract art

00:11:11.190 --> 00:11:13.100
the common denominator argument

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:13.933
and he finds it

00:11:13.933 --> 00:11:16.160
the most persuasive of the autonomists.

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:18.500
Basically, we must be a able to apply

00:11:18.500 --> 00:11:20.790
our criterion for evaluating art

00:11:20.790 --> 00:11:22.340
to all kinds of art.

00:11:22.340 --> 00:11:23.517
But as he explains,

00:11:23.517 --> 00:11:24.417
"Since it is the case

00:11:24.417 --> 00:11:25.647
"that much art does not

00:11:25.647 --> 00:11:27.387
"concern morality directly,

00:11:27.387 --> 00:11:29.957
"ethics cannot be the
appropriate criterion

00:11:29.957 --> 00:11:31.390
"for evaluating art."

00:11:31.390 --> 00:11:32.940
If you can't apply moral judgment

00:11:32.940 --> 00:11:35.090
to every piece of art, that is,

00:11:35.090 --> 00:11:36.850
why should you apply it to any?

00:11:36.850 --> 00:11:39.160
That said, we see the moralist point

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:40.117
when it comes to things like

00:11:40.117 --> 00:11:41.170
"Triumph of the Will"

00:11:41.170 --> 00:11:42.740
or "Birth of a Nation,"

00:11:42.740 --> 00:11:44.480
so we're left pretty torn.

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:45.804
But before you despair,

00:11:45.804 --> 00:11:47.120
there's one viewpoint

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:49.560
that offers a life saving curve ball.

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:50.393
And it was given

00:11:50.393 --> 00:11:52.300
by writer and philosopher, Iris Murdoch.

00:11:52.300 --> 00:11:54.520
For Murdoch, the function of the best art

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:56.290
is to pierce the veil

00:11:56.290 --> 00:11:57.220
and let us see things

00:11:57.220 --> 00:11:59.010
as they truly are.

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:00.220
But for her,

00:12:00.220 --> 00:12:02.930
this vision is inseparable from virtue

00:12:02.930 --> 00:12:05.060
and to convey a truthful
vision of the world,

00:12:05.060 --> 00:12:07.400
the artist needs to
understand human virtue.

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:10.350
Truth then becomes the real morality.

00:12:10.350 --> 00:12:12.390
Murdoch believed that the best art

00:12:12.390 --> 00:12:14.220
holds up a mirror to reality,

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:15.340
including, and in particular,

00:12:15.340 --> 00:12:17.460
moral reality.

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:18.740
But if an artist wants us

00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:21.080
to better understand reality and morality,

00:12:21.080 --> 00:12:22.610
they need to understand it

00:12:22.610 --> 00:12:23.730
in the first place.

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:24.563
Under this thinking,

00:12:24.563 --> 00:12:26.370
"Triumph of the Will
and "Birth of a Nation"

00:12:26.370 --> 00:12:28.960
can't possibly be considered
great works of art

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:29.800
'cause they don't show

00:12:29.800 --> 00:12:31.180
a true picture of the world.

00:12:31.180 --> 00:12:33.830
They're both functionally
racist fever dreams.

00:12:33.830 --> 00:12:36.780
Philosopher AH Hannay
takes it one step further,

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:37.800
arguing that art

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:40.607
can even push a society's
morality forward.

00:12:40.607 --> 00:12:42.477
"Art is then seen throughout history

00:12:42.477 --> 00:12:44.097
"to be continually helping

00:12:44.097 --> 00:12:45.677
"in the creation of new values,

00:12:45.677 --> 00:12:48.150
"in the transvaluation of values."

00:12:48.150 --> 00:12:49.240
Like how "Get Out"

00:12:49.240 --> 00:12:50.450
can make us think about the way

00:12:50.450 --> 00:12:51.910
white liberals contribute

00:12:51.910 --> 00:12:53.060
to systematic racism,

00:12:53.060 --> 00:12:54.170
or how "Parasite"

00:12:54.170 --> 00:12:55.900
can make us rethink inequality,

00:12:55.900 --> 00:12:57.870
or how "First Reformed"

00:12:57.870 --> 00:13:00.641
reframes the environmental
justice movement to ask,

00:13:00.641 --> 00:13:02.510
"Should a drunk, horny priest

00:13:02.510 --> 00:13:03.690
blow up his church?"

00:13:03.690 --> 00:13:05.850
Now, without committing to moralism,

00:13:05.850 --> 00:13:07.050
let's entertain the notion

00:13:07.050 --> 00:13:09.250
that the ethical content of a work

00:13:09.250 --> 00:13:11.710
is directly relevant to its value as art.

00:13:11.710 --> 00:13:12.720
If that's true,

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:15.440
it's natural to ask whether a piece of art

00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:17.730
could help us see the world more clearly

00:13:17.730 --> 00:13:20.570
by openly depicting immoral content.

00:13:20.570 --> 00:13:22.040
Some widely acclaimed films

00:13:22.040 --> 00:13:23.930
about war and organized crime

00:13:23.930 --> 00:13:24.840
might fit the bill,

00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:26.890
or 90s rap that gave us

00:13:26.890 --> 00:13:28.240
an unflinching picture

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:29.670
of what life was like in places

00:13:29.670 --> 00:13:30.970
like Queens or Compton.

00:13:30.970 --> 00:13:32.840
It's hard to imagine how Illmatic

00:13:32.840 --> 00:13:33.980
could have been improved

00:13:33.980 --> 00:13:37.180
by a cleaner, more
unproblematic moral stance

00:13:37.180 --> 00:13:39.930
or that "The Godfather"
would've been better art

00:13:39.930 --> 00:13:42.749
if nobody had played
pranks with horse heads.

00:13:42.749 --> 00:13:45.590
(screaming)

00:13:45.590 --> 00:13:47.427
Philosopher John Stuart Mill said that,

00:13:47.427 --> 00:13:49.267
"He who knows only his own side

00:13:49.267 --> 00:13:50.897
"of the case knows little of that.

00:13:50.897 --> 00:13:52.257
"His reasons may be good

00:13:52.257 --> 00:13:54.267
"and no one may have
been able to refute them.

00:13:54.267 --> 00:13:56.557
"But if he is equally
unable to refute the reasons

00:13:56.557 --> 00:13:57.758
"on the opposite side,

00:13:57.758 --> 00:14:00.617
"if he does not so much
as know what they are,

00:14:00.617 --> 00:14:03.850
"he has no ground for
preferring either opinion."

00:14:03.850 --> 00:14:04.683
That is to say,

00:14:04.683 --> 00:14:07.680
if we only see our characters
doing moral things,

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:10.210
we never get to experience
immorality in art

00:14:10.210 --> 00:14:12.430
and we can't truly call ourselves

00:14:12.430 --> 00:14:14.360
fully morally engaged.

00:14:14.360 --> 00:14:16.260
That means there could be value

00:14:16.260 --> 00:14:18.890
in art that represents immoral activities,

00:14:18.890 --> 00:14:20.000
if and only if,

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:23.460
it shows us a true
perspective on the world

00:14:23.460 --> 00:14:25.250
as laid out by Iris Murdoch.

00:14:25.250 --> 00:14:26.361
And if it's a novel one,

00:14:26.361 --> 00:14:28.070
all the better, right?

00:14:28.070 --> 00:14:30.570
Of course, this is not
without complication.

00:14:30.570 --> 00:14:33.009
For example, the film
"Scarface" is, for many,

00:14:33.009 --> 00:14:35.870
a parable about how you
shouldn't solve arguments

00:14:35.870 --> 00:14:36.880
with machine guns.

00:14:36.880 --> 00:14:38.520
But for some of the rappers featured

00:14:38.520 --> 00:14:40.369
on MTV Cribs in the early 00s,

00:14:40.369 --> 00:14:42.760
it's an aspirational tale.

00:14:42.760 --> 00:14:44.460
What are we to make of that?

00:14:44.460 --> 00:14:45.940
The honest answer might be

00:14:45.940 --> 00:14:47.350
that as an artist depicting

00:14:47.350 --> 00:14:49.260
morally questionable behavior,

00:14:49.260 --> 00:14:51.980
you do not and cannot have total control

00:14:51.980 --> 00:14:54.110
over how people interpret your work.

00:14:54.110 --> 00:14:57.020
What's more, morality can be relative.

00:14:57.020 --> 00:14:58.730
Thus, what does and does not

00:14:58.730 --> 00:15:01.740
constitute immoral art can be fluid.

00:15:01.740 --> 00:15:03.870
And that makes the task
of morally evaluating

00:15:03.870 --> 00:15:05.970
these works murky at best.

00:15:05.970 --> 00:15:07.579
However, because immorality

00:15:07.579 --> 00:15:09.320
does exist in the world,

00:15:09.320 --> 00:15:10.900
portraying it truthfully could,

00:15:10.900 --> 00:15:13.700
paradoxically, be a moral thing to do.

00:15:13.700 --> 00:15:14.880
And what's more,

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:16.441
works that intentionally or not

00:15:16.441 --> 00:15:18.380
display immorality

00:15:18.380 --> 00:15:20.980
can also be highly useful to engage with,

00:15:20.980 --> 00:15:24.250
even if they only reveal
our own shortcomings.

00:15:24.250 --> 00:15:25.609
And we think evaluating

00:15:25.609 --> 00:15:27.550
the immoral virtue of art

00:15:27.550 --> 00:15:30.200
is perhaps the crux of
this media conflict.

00:15:30.200 --> 00:15:31.570
People who criticize "Joker"

00:15:31.570 --> 00:15:32.970
don't typically believe

00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:33.900
that the film is endorsing

00:15:33.900 --> 00:15:35.350
the Joker's actions.

00:15:35.350 --> 00:15:37.580
They believe that the very depiction

00:15:37.580 --> 00:15:39.430
of those actions is dangerous.

00:15:39.430 --> 00:15:41.970
But if "Joker" shows wild clown antics

00:15:41.970 --> 00:15:42.900
for the purpose of, say,

00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:44.700
commenting on the scourge

00:15:44.700 --> 00:15:46.350
that is America's underfunded

00:15:46.350 --> 00:15:47.567
mental healthcare system,

00:15:47.567 --> 00:15:49.420
then could its immorality

00:15:49.420 --> 00:15:51.040
have a moral purpose?

00:15:51.040 --> 00:15:52.650
To go back to Wesley Morris,

00:15:52.650 --> 00:15:54.340
When we expect all of our art

00:15:54.340 --> 00:15:55.830
to be morally upstanding,

00:15:55.830 --> 00:15:57.210
there's a real consequence.

00:15:57.210 --> 00:15:59.917
He writes, "We wind up with safer art,

00:15:59.917 --> 00:16:02.137
"and discourse that provokes and disturbs,

00:16:02.137 --> 00:16:03.357
"and shocks less.

00:16:03.357 --> 00:16:05.507
"It gives us culture whose artistic value

00:16:05.507 --> 00:16:07.757
"has been replaced by moral judgment

00:16:07.757 --> 00:16:09.500
"and leaves us with monocriticism."

00:16:09.500 --> 00:16:11.890
So maybe the real answer is that,

00:16:11.890 --> 00:16:13.820
yes, art should be moral.

00:16:13.820 --> 00:16:15.440
But its main moral obligation

00:16:15.440 --> 00:16:17.520
is to be truthful about the world.

00:16:17.520 --> 00:16:18.370
In which case,

00:16:18.370 --> 00:16:21.018
art that shows the world's
immorality truthfully

00:16:21.018 --> 00:16:23.050
is a bit, ironically,

00:16:23.050 --> 00:16:25.440
A-OK in terms of morality.

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:26.740
But what do you guys think?

00:16:26.740 --> 00:16:27.850
Are the autonomists right

00:16:27.850 --> 00:16:29.700
that ethics has no role to play

00:16:29.700 --> 00:16:30.970
in evaluating art?

00:16:30.970 --> 00:16:32.700
Are moralists like Tolstoy right

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:34.990
that immoral art makes us hedonists?

00:16:34.990 --> 00:16:37.290
Is morality in art really just truth.

00:16:37.290 --> 00:16:39.200
And if so, can immoral art

00:16:39.200 --> 00:16:41.130
actually be totally moral?

00:16:41.130 --> 00:16:43.200
Let us know what you
think in the comments.

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:45.140
Big thanks to our patrons
for all your support

00:16:45.140 --> 00:16:47.780
and be sure to stop by
our updated Patreon page.

00:16:47.780 --> 00:16:49.800
And don't forget to
check us out on TikTok.

00:16:49.800 --> 00:16:51.090
Clutch that subscribe button

00:16:51.090 --> 00:16:52.720
like it's Tipper Gore's string of pearls

00:16:52.720 --> 00:16:54.870
and don't forget to ring that bell.

00:16:54.870 --> 00:16:55.703
and as always,

00:16:55.703 --> 00:16:57.161
thanks for watching, later.

00:16:57.161 --> 00:16:59.744
